  "notes": "Guitar-player's country — a neotraditional band arranged around a virtuoso Telecaster. Keys are a stated consequence of the instrument: \"guitar keys (E/A/G/D) chosen for open-string Tele licks.\" The kit is arranged around the guitar too, and the entry says so as a rule: \"the kit stays out of the guitar's register — snare tuned high, cymbals restrained, so the Tele's upper-mid content reads,\" which is a low perc gain and a restrained hat fill against a two-beat with quarter kick under choruses. The bass rule is quoted: \"the bass and kick lock on 1 and 3 to leave the backbeat clear for guitar accents.\" The single most important parameter is the countermelody, because the entry states outright that \"the lead guitar is a second lead vocalist\" — so this model carries the highest counter density ratio in the section, and the arrangement gives the solo slot \"full 16- or 24-bar solo sections rather than 8-bar ones\" plus \"an instrumental outro that often runs longer than the intro.\" Harmony is the stated I–IV–V with V7, I–I7–IV blues turns and a ♭VII rock borrow; the sevenths live in `extensions` because the numeral parser reads degrees only. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium): \"the guitar-grammar side is directly sourced; harmony generalised [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the counter ratio, the perc gain and the velocity tiers.",
